          "The third Russia-Africa Summit opened in Moscow on 28 October 2026 with an economic and humanitarian forum, followed by a leaders' session at the Grand Kremlin Palace on 29 October. It is co-chaired by Vladimir Putin and Burundi's President Évariste Ndayishimiye, the current chair of the African Union (TASS; Al Jazeera, 28 October 2026). According to Kremlin foreign policy aide Yuri Ushakov, 48 of Africa's 54 countries are represented, 22 of them at head-of-state level (RIA Novosti, 26 October 2026; Reuters, 29 October 2026). The summit comes as the war in Ukraine enters its fifth year, as talks between Russia, the United States and Ukraine are under way in Abu Dhabi (Interfax, 28 October 2026), and after Russia's military presence in Africa grew from four to nine countries since early 2023 (I. Kłyszcz, Russia-Africa Monitor, 4 October 2026).",
