          "The analysis draws on several frameworks. Hirschman (1945) shows that one country's political influence over another stems less from the volume of trade than from its asymmetry and the cost of interrupting it. David (1991) introduced the idea of omnibalancing: in many states, leaders choose external alignments first to protect themselves against internal threats. Kuik (2008) describes hedging, through which middle-sized states avoid choosing sides while seeking gains from each. Cooley and Nexon (2020) explain how the emergence of alternative patrons gives smaller states exit options in the face of Western conditions. Bayart (2000) uses the term extraversion for the way African elites mobilise external resources to serve domestic strategies. Finally, Fearon (1997) distinguishes costly signals, which bind the sender, from cheap announcements, which bind no one. This version, updated on 30 October, incorporates the “Moscow Declaration” adopted on 29 October and published that evening on the Kremlin website; its full text could only be consulted through press reports."
