          "The framework was set by Presidential Decree No. 194 of 25 March 2026, which scheduled a summit in Moscow in autumn 2026 and put Yuri Ushakov in charge of its organisation (Vedomosti, 26 March 2026). This broke with the stated intention in 2024 of holding the summit in Africa. In February 2024, the head of the Russian foreign ministry's Africa department, Vsevolod Tkachenko, said it would take place “on the African continent” (TASS, 13 February 2024), and Equatorial Guinea had offered to host it in Malabo (TASS, 26 September 2024). Chatham House says Libya was first considered (T. Amare, 29 October 2026). No official explanation for the move to Moscow has been found. One hypothesis can be advanced: a summit in Moscow reduces logistical and security risks for the Russian president, who has been the subject of an International Criminal Court arrest warrant since March 2023, and allows him to receive each leader on his own ground.",
