          "The second condition is to negotiate collectively wherever possible. In 2006 the Banjul formula provided for around fifteen leaders to represent Africa at these summits; it is widely ignored (ISS, PSC Report, 13 December 2019). In 2023 William Ruto complained that African leaders were “loaded into buses like school children” (The Star, 17 May 2023). The Moscow meeting with ten regional organisations, and the memoranda with the EAC, COMESA and the AfCFTA Secretariat announced by Russian diplomats (RT, 28 October 2026), point to a way forward: negotiating market access, rules of origin and standards at AfCFTA level rather than country by country. President Ndayishimiye himself set four priorities for the 2027-2029 plan: productive investment, faster infrastructure and agriculture projects, technology transfer and direct market access (Sputnik, 29 October 2026).",
          "The third condition is to tie each agreement to a measurable contribution to productive transformation. Fertiliser and nuclear power offer the opportunity. A country importing Russian fertiliser can negotiate multi-year contracts with indexed prices and local blending or production units; a country signing with Rosatom can require publication of the financing terms, a local content share and the training of national engineers, drawing on what Egypt negotiated for El Dabaa. The technology transfer the AU presidency is asking for cannot be decreed in a declaration; it has to be written into verifiable contractual clauses.",
          "The fourth condition concerns security. Military agreements would benefit from being submitted to national parliaments and from including clauses on compliance with international humanitarian law, arms traceability and the protection of nationals, particularly against recruitment into the Russian army. The fifth is to preserve diversification itself: the value of the Russian option lies in adding to other partnerships rather than replacing them. A state that locks itself into a single, security-centred and narrowly based relationship loses the benefit of competition among partners that Cooley and Nexon (2020) describe."
