          "Between 1 and 25 October 2026, Ivorian farmers delivered 122,453 tonnes of cocoa to the ports, against 215,219 tonnes over the same period a year earlier, a 43% drop (Bloomberg, via Barchart, 26 and 27 October 2026). On 4 October, Ivorian authorities seized 492 bags, or 42.2 tonnes, near Ebilassokro on their way to Ghana (CocoaIntel, 6 October 2026). The two facts have partly different causes, but they point to the same gap. For the 2026/27 main crop, the guaranteed farmgate price is 1,200 CFA francs per kilo in Côte d'Ivoire (Reuters, 1 September 2026) and 42,400 cedis per tonne in Ghana, or about 2,100 CFA francs per kilo (Koaci, 28 September 2026). A Ghanaian grower is therefore paid about 75% more than a neighbour across the border for beans of comparable quality.",
          "This note treats the gap as a regional value chain problem rather than a customs story. The two countries supply about 60% of world output (Côte d'Ivoire-Ghana Cocoa Initiative, 22 June 2026), committed on 16 June 2026 to harmonise farmgate prices, and both want to process more beans at home. The analysis looks at where the gap comes from, what it does along the border and in the processing industry, and what it reveals about the conditions for credible coordination. Several references frame the discussion. Bates (1981) showed how African marketing boards extract rents by setting domestic prices below world prices, and how farmers respond by exiting into parallel channels. Bhagwati and Hansen (1973) model smuggling as arbitrage whose scale depends on the price gap net of transport costs and the risk of seizure. Stigler (1964) shows that an agreement among sellers holds only if deviations from the common rule can be observed and are costly to whoever deviates."
