          "In Ghana, Law No. 1182 of 2026 guarantees farmers at least 70% of the gross FOB price and provides for periodic price reviews (Koaci, 28 September 2026; Reuters, 28 August 2026). Indexation passes a price recovery on to farmers faster, but it has to be financed. The syndicated pre-financing loans that used to fund purchases broke down in 2023/24 (GBC, 8 October 2026), and COCOBOD now borrows on the domestic market. In early October its subsidiary Cocoa Capital PLC raised 3.39 billion cedis, about $288 million, at 11% maturing in June 2027, against a target of 4 billion. This is the first tranche of a 16.3 billion cedi programme (Sika Finance and GBC, 8 October 2026). Licensed buyers said in September that COCOBOD still owed them about 4 billion cedis for the previous season (Reuters, via Food Business Africa, 24 September 2026).",
          "The two systems allocate price risk differently. Newbery and Stiglitz (1981) showed that stabilising producer prices has value for poorly diversified households, but that its cost depends on who bears the residual risk and how well they can finance it. The Ivorian system shields the regulator from a price fall after the sale, but leaves farmers with the shortfall when prices rise after the sale. The Ghanaian system passes price increases through to farmers, but exposes the board to liquidity risk, which it now covers with short-term domestic debt. Neither is better in all circumstances. The gap arises because the two countries sold at different points in a very wide price cycle, which is exactly what the calendar harmonisation announced in June was meant to prevent. This reading is an interpretation of the published rules; the details of the CCC's contracts are not public."
