          "For a grinder based in Côte d'Ivoire, a low domestic price is in principle a cost advantage. It is one only if the beans stay in the country and can be documented. In the framework set out by Gereffi, Humphrey and Sturgeon (2005), the way a chain is governed depends on how far buyers can codify and verify product attributes. Origin and deforestation-free status have become such attributes. The EU Deforestation Regulation (EUDR) is due to apply to large and medium operators from 30 December 2026 (John Owan Enoh, Nigeria's Minister of State for Industry, Arise News, 15 July 2026), and the EU accounts for between half and 60% of world cocoa imports depending on the source (BusinessDay, 11 July 2026; Africtelegraph, 16 September 2026). To comply, the CCC made a chip-based producer card mandatory for 2026/27 (Futura, 24 September 2026). A bean that crosses the border illegally loses its origin record. It can re-enter the Ghanaian system, but only with an inaccurate declaration of origin. The price gap thus becomes a compliance risk for both countries, not just a revenue risk for one of them.",
          "Timing makes this worse. In August, CCC and exporter sources expected about 900,000 tonnes to reach the ports between October and December, against 1.1 million a year earlier, with exports bunched in December just before the regulation takes effect (Reuters, 27 August 2026). If October deliveries stay 43% below last year, the logistical pressure will shift to the final weeks of the year, precisely when every lot will need documentation. Ivorian grinders, who need a steady supply to run their plants, are as exposed to this delay as bean exporters. The same logic applies in Nigeria, where investors are building a 70,000-tonne plant in Sagamu due to be commissioned in 2027 (BusinessDay, 11 July 2026): a local processing strategy requires a domestic price high enough to keep the beans at home."
