          "Stigler (1964) helps explain this fragility. A coordination agreement holds when each party can observe the other's conduct and when deviating from the rule is costly for the deviator. The June declaration sets a harmonisation goal without a common formula: on one side a price indexed to at least 70% of FOB, on the other a price tied to forward sales and a 60% share of CIF. Each country can follow its own rule while producing a price very different from its neighbour's, so neither formally breaches the agreement. According to the Ivorian paper Le Moment, Abidjan has broken the June commitment; according to Notre Afrik, no formal complaint had been lodged by 30 September (both cited by Afrique Confidentielle, 3 October 2026). These conflicting readings reflect the lack of a verifiable harmonisation criterion.",
          "Yet the two countries have applied common rules before when those rules were simple and public. The Living Income Differential of $400 per tonne has applied to sales from both origins since the 2020/21 season, and the two boards have published their origin differentials every month since May 2022 (CIGCI, 31 October 2023). Credible farmgate harmonisation would require a rule of the same kind: a common share of the FOB price, a common reference window for forward sales and simultaneous mid-season reviews. It would also require a financing instrument able to absorb the difference between forward-sale prices and spot prices. Otherwise the country that sells early will remain exposed, every time prices recover, to what Bates (1981) described as farmers' ordinary response to implicit taxation: exit. Where the border is porous, that exit takes the form of supply shifting to the neighbour."
